# One airbrake, no steering: the scenario that can see the airbrake yaw term's
# SIGN.
#
# `airbrake-asymmetric.inputs` exists to measure the term's *magnitude*, and it
# deliberately mirrors brake and steering onto the same side so that
# `|airbrake_l - airbrake_r|` can be read independently of any sign convention.
# That is exactly what makes it blind to the sign: the steering drive is several
# times the larger of the two, so a run with the airbrake yaw inverted still
# curves the way the stick asks and differs only in rate. A reimplementation can
# have the term backwards and reproduce that capture's direction on both halves.
#
# This scenario holds `steer == 0` for its whole length, so nothing else in the
# force list can yaw the craft:
#
#     angularLocal.y += fs * Airbrake.turn * (R - L) * 0.001
#
# is the only drive, and the sign of the resulting rotation is the sign of that
# term. `L` alone must turn the nose **toward** the braked side, which in the
# recorded basis (whose row 0 is the ship's left - see engine.md, "The basis is
# positively oriented, and row 0 points left") means `forward` rotating toward
# `+row0`.
#
# It is deliberately single-sided rather than a left/right sweep: a mirrored pair
# in one run shares a start pose and a stretch of track, so an asymmetry in the
# geometry reads as an asymmetry in the law. Capture `r` in a second run from the
# same restart if the mirror is wanted.
#
# The first 60 ticks are thrust alone, because all three airbrake terms carry the
# cached speed `craft+0x2ec` as a factor and a standing craft gets nothing from
# any of them. The last 50 are the release, which is also the within-capture
# control: the same speed, the same stretch, no brake.
#
#     just scripted-emu verification/scenarios/airbrake-left-only.inputs \
#         data/traces/talons-junction-airbrake-left-only.csv
#
# Reference scenario: Time Trial, Venom, Talon's Junction White, Assegai.

60 cross
90 cross l
50 cross
